## Why Asked
Tests understanding of graph traversal fundamentals, algorithm selection, and space-time tradeoffs in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
**BFS (Breadth-First Search)**: Uses queue, explores neighbors level-by-level. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(V) worst case (all nodes in queue). Guarantees shortest path in unweighted graphs.

**DFS (Depth-First Search)**: Uses stack or recursion, explores as deep as possible before backtracking. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(h) where h is maximum depth (recursion stack), O(V) worst case for skewed graphs.

## When to Use Each
**BFS**: Shortest path routing, social network friend suggestions, web crawlers finding closest pages, level-order tree traversals.

**DFS**: Topological sorting, cycle detection, maze solving, finding connected components, backtracking problems like Sudoku or N-Queens.

## Code Example
```javascript
// BFS - finds shortest path
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const distances = { [start]: 0 };

  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor);
        distances[neighbor] = distances[node] + 1;
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return distances;
}

// DFS - recursive implementation
function dfs(graph, node, visited = new Set(), result = []) {
  if (visited.has(node)) return result;

  visited.add(node);
  result.push(node);

  for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
    dfs(graph, neighbor, visited, result);
  }

  return result;
}

// DFS - iterative implementation
function dfsIterative(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (visited.has(node)) continue;

    visited.add(node);
    result.push(node);

    // Add neighbors to stack (reverse for consistent order)
    for (let i = graph[node].length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const neighbor = graph[node][i];
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        stack.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Memory Considerations
BFS can consume significant memory for wide graphs (queue holds all nodes at current level). DFS is memory-efficient for deep, narrow graphs but may cause stack overflow for very deep recursion. Choose based on graph structure and problem requirements.

## Why This Is Asked

Standard quicksort performs well on average but can degrade to O(n²) on adversarial inputs, particularly with many duplicates. Introsort-style hybrids combine the practical efficiency of quicksort with worst-case guarantees, making-world engineering this a common real constraint.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)**: Partitions array into elements less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot, dramatically reducing comparisons for datasets with many duplicates
- **Recursion depth tracking**: Prevents stack overflow and performance degradation on pathological inputs
- **Hybrid switching**: Falls back to mergesort or heapsort when depth exceeds 2*log2(N) or partition size drops below a threshold (~16 elements)
- **Worst-case guarantees**: Ensures O(n log n) time complexity regardless of input distribution

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  function quickSort3Way(lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo < 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }
    if (depth > maxDepth) {
      mergeSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = partition3Way(arr, lo, hi);
    quickSort3Way(lo, lt - 1, depth + 1);
    quickSort3Way(gt + 1, hi, depth + 1);
  }

  quickSort3Way(0, n - 1, 0);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with dual objectives: minimize cost and count optimal deletion sets, plus implement in two styles. It also covers edge cases like empty substrings and ties.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings; store (cost, ways).
- Memoization and bottom-up parallels; careful counting on ties.
- Modulo arithmetic for counts.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Memoized DFS
function solveMemo(s, delCost){
  const n = s.length;
  const memoCost = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(-1));
  const memoWays = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  function dfs(l,r){
    if (l>=r) return {cost:0, ways:1};
    if (memoCost[l][r]!==-1) return {cost:memoCost[l][r], ways:memoWays[l][r]};
    if (s[l]===s[r]){
      const res = dfs(l+1,r-1);
      memoCost[l][r]=res.cost;
      memoWays[l][r]=res.ways;
      return res;
    } else {
      const A = dfs(l+1,r);
      const B = dfs(l,r-1);
      let cost;
      let ways = 0;
      if (A.cost + delCost[l] < B.cost + delCost[r]){
        cost = A.cost + delCost[l];
        ways = A.ways;
      } else if (B.cost + delCost[r] < A.cost + delCost[l]){
        cost = B.cost + delCost[r];
        ways = B.ways;
      } else {
        cost = A.cost + Math.min(delCost[l], delCost[r]);
        ways = (A.ways + B.ways) % MOD;
      }
      memoCost[l][r]=cost; memoWays[l][r]=ways;
      return {cost, ways};
    }
  }
  return dfs(0,n-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of a Trie, basic operations, and how to implement autocomplete with limited results.

## Key Concepts
- Trie nodes with children and isEnd
- DFS traversal for autocomplete
- Lexicographic ordering via alphabetical traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }
  search(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return false;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.isEnd;
  }
  startsWith(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return false;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return true;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, limit) {
    let node = this.root; const path = [];
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      path.push(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    function dfs(n, acc) {
      if (results.length >= limit) return;
      if (n.isEnd) results.push(prefix + acc.join(''));
      const keys = Array.from(n.children.keys()).sort();
      for (const c of keys) {
        acc.push(c);
        dfs(n.children.get(c), acc);
        acc.pop();
      }
    }
    dfs(node, []);
    return results;
  }
}
```

